\begin_layout Subsubsection
Space Charge & The Center Of The Rod-Conductor (
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{r}_{2}$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to construct the position vector of the spherical image charge,
 we need both the distance & the direction along the line that connects the position of the infinitesimally differential space charge 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:space_charge_pos"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 and the center of the rod tip (
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{C}=-R\hat{z}$
\end_inset

).
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{C}$
\end_inset

 denotes the position of the center and is not the constant 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 of the density function 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:space_charge_density"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

.
 This can be achieved as:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{cc}
\vec{r}_{2}= & \vec{P}_{dq_{sp}}-\vec{C}\\
\vec{r}_{2}= & rsin(\theta)cos(\phi)\hat{x}+rsin(\theta)sin(\phi)\hat{y}+rcos(\theta)\hat{z}-\left(-R\hat{z}\right)\\
\vec{r}_{2}= & rsin(\theta)cos(\phi)\hat{x}+rsin(\theta)sin(\phi)\hat{y}+\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]\hat{z}
\end{array}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
while its magnitude (length):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\paragraph_spacing double
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{cc}
r_{2}= & \sqrt{r^{2}sin^{2}(\theta)cos^{2}(\phi)+r^{2}sin^{2}(\theta)sin^{2}(\phi)+\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]^{2}}\\
r_{2}= & \sqrt{r^{2}sin^{2}(\theta)\left[\cancelto{1}{cos^{2}(\phi)+sin^{2}(\phi)}\right]+\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]^{2}}\\
r_{2}= & \sqrt{\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]^{2}+\left[rsin(\theta)\right]^{2}}
\end{array}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and its unit direction:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{r}_{2}=\frac{\vec{r}_{2}}{r_{2}}=\frac{rsin(\theta)cos(\phi)\hat{x}+rsin(\theta)sin(\phi)\hat{y}+\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]\hat{z}}{\sqrt{\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]^{2}+\left[rsin(\theta)\right]^{2}}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Image Charge Position (
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{P}_{dq'}$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
According to the Method of 
\bar under
Images for a differential point charge in the presence of a grounded spherical conductor
\bar default
,
 the image charge (
\begin_inset Formula $dq'$
\end_inset

) is located at a distance (
\begin_inset Formula $r_{3}$
\end_inset

) away from the center of the conductor:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{3}=\frac{R^{2}}{r_{2}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
where
\bar default
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

:
 Is the radius of the spherical conductor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $r_{2}$
\end_inset

:
 Is the distance between the differential point charge & the center of the spherical conductor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To maintain absolute theoretical alignment with the analytical framework established by 
\series bold
Wang Wenduan & Wang Haiting [1]
\series default
,
 it is necessary to formally define the localized polar angle (
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{3}$
\end_inset

).
 This angle characterizes the orientation of the alignment vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{r}_{2}$
\end_inset

 relative to the central vertical z-axis of symmetry.While tracking individual Cartesian coordinates introduces an explicit azimuthal dependency via 
\begin_inset Formula $\cos(\phi)$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\sin(\phi)$
\end_inset

,
 the inherent axisymmetry of the rod-plane gap dictates that the localized polar angle depends globally on the absolute radial displacement away from the vertical axis.
 By evaluating the norm of the total projection within the horizontal 
\begin_inset Formula $x\text{-}y$
\end_inset

 plane,
 the azimuthal parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 is analytically eliminated via the fundamental circular identity 
\begin_inset Formula $\cos^{2}(\phi)+\sin^{2}(\phi)=1$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\paragraph_spacing double
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{cc}
sin(\theta_{3})= & \frac{\left\Vert \left(\vec{r}_{2}\cdot\hat{x}\right)\hat{x}+\left(\vec{r}_{2}\cdot\hat{y}\right)\hat{y}\right\Vert }{r_{2}}=\frac{\sqrt{\left[rsin(\theta)cos(\phi)\right]^{2}+\left[rsin(\theta)sin(\phi)\right]^{2}}}{r_{2}}=\frac{\sqrt{r^{2}sin^{2}(\theta)\left[\cancelto{1}{cos^{2}(\phi)+sin^{2}(\phi)}\right]}}{r_{2}}\\
sin(\theta_{3})= & \frac{rsin(\theta)}{r_{2}}\Rightarrow rsin(\theta)=r_{2}sin(\theta_{3})\\
\theta_{3}= & sin^{-1}\left(\frac{rsin(\theta)}{r_{2}}\right)
\end{array}\label{eq:rod_image_sin_of_theta_3}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
while:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
cos(\theta_{3})=\frac{\vec{r}_{2}\cdot\hat{z}}{r_{2}}=\frac{R+rcos(\theta)}{r_{2}}\label{eq:rod_image_cos_of_theta_3}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The precise position vector that can describe the exact location of the differential image charge can be constructed as:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\paragraph_spacing double
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{cc}
\vec{P}_{dq'}= & \vec{C}+r_{3}\hat{r}_{2}=-R\hat{z}+r_{3}\frac{rsin(\theta)cos(\phi)\hat{x}+rsin(\theta)sin(\phi)\hat{y}+\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]\hat{z}}{r_{2}}\\
\vec{P}_{dq'}= & \frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)cos(\phi)\hat{x}+\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)sin(\phi)\hat{y}+\left\{ \frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]-R\right\} \hat{z}
\end{array}\label{eq:rod_image_position}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Image Charge To Observation Displacement Vector (
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{r}_{4}$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 the 
\bar under
Image Charge To Observation Displacement Vector
\bar default
 can be calculated as:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\paragraph_spacing double
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{cc}
\vec{r}_{4}= & \vec{P}_{obs}-\vec{P}_{dq'}\\
\vec{r}_{4}= & z\hat{z}-\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)cos(\phi)\hat{x}-\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)sin(\phi)\hat{y}-\left\{ \frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]-R\right\} \hat{z}\\
\vec{r}_{4}= & -\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)cos(\phi)\hat{x}-\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)sin(\phi)\hat{y}-\left\{ \frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]-R-z\right\} \hat{z}
\end{array}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and the angle of the orientation of the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{r}_{4}$
\end_inset

 relative to the central vertical z-axis of symmetry:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\paragraph_spacing double
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{cc}
sin(\theta_{4})= & \frac{\left\Vert \left(\vec{r}_{4}\cdot\hat{x}\right)\hat{x}+\left(\vec{r}_{4}\cdot\hat{y}\right)\hat{y}\right\Vert }{r_{4}}=\frac{\sqrt{\left[\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)cos(\phi)\right]^{2}+\left[\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)sin(\phi)\right]^{2}}}{r_{4}}\\
sin(\theta_{4})= & \frac{\sqrt{\left(\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}\right)^{2}r^{2}sin^{2}(\theta)\left[\cancelto{1}{cos^{2}(\phi)+sin^{2}(\phi)}\right]}}{r_{4}}=\frac{\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)}{r_{4}}=\frac{r_{3}\cdot\cancelto{sin(\theta_{3})}{\frac{rsin(\theta)}{r_{2}}}}{r_{4}}\\
\theta_{4}= & sin^{-1}\left(\frac{r_{3}sin(\theta_{3})}{r_{4}}\right)
\end{array}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Projecting 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{r}_{4}$
\end_inset

 on the axis of symmetry yields:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\paragraph_spacing double
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{cc}
cos(\theta_{4})= & \frac{\vec{r}_{4}\cdot\hat{z}}{r_{4}}\\
cos(\theta_{4})= & \frac{z+R-\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]}{r_{4}}=\frac{z+R-r_{3}\cancelto{cos(\theta_{3})}{\frac{\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]}{r_{2}}}}{r_{4}}\\
cos(\theta_{4})= & \frac{z+R-r_{3}cos(\theta_{3})}{r_{4}}
\end{array}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
while its magnitude (length):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\paragraph_spacing double
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{cc}
r_{4}= & \sqrt{\left[\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)cos(\phi)\right]^{2}+\left[\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)sin(\phi)\right]^{2}+\left(\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]-R-z\right)^{2}}\\
r_{4}= & \sqrt{\left(\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)\right)^{2}\left[\cancelto{1}{cos^{2}(\phi)+sin^{2}(\phi)}\right]+\left(\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]-R-z\right)^{2}}\\
r_{4}= & \sqrt{\left(\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)\right)^{2}+\left(\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]-R-z\right)^{2}}
\end{array}\label{eq:rod_image_r4}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and since 
\begin_inset Formula $rsin(\theta)=r_{2}sin(\theta_{3})$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:rod_image_sin_of_theta_3"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 & 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{R+rcos(\theta)}{r_{2}}=cos(\theta_{3})$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:rod_image_cos_of_theta_3"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 substituting them into 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:rod_image_r4"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 we get:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{cc}
r_{4}= & \sqrt{\left(\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}r_{2}sin(\theta_{3})\right)^{2}+\left\{ r_{3}cos(\theta_{3})-R-z\right\} ^{2}}\\
r_{4}= & \sqrt{\left[z+R-r_{3}cos(\theta_{3})\right]^{2}+\left[r_{3}sin(\theta_{3})\right]^{2}}
\end{array}\label{eq:image_charge_r4_final}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and the unit direction:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{r}_{4}=\frac{\vec{r}_{4}}{r_{4}}=\frac{-\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)cos(\phi)\hat{x}-\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)sin(\phi)\hat{y}-\left\{ \frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]-R-z\right\} \hat{z}}{r_{4}}\label{eq:r4_direction}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Final Calculation Of The Conductor Image Electric Field (
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
According to the Method of 
\bar under
Images for a differential point charge in the presence of a grounded spherical conductor
\bar default
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
dq'=-\frac{R}{r_{2}}dq_{sp}\label{eq:image_charge_dq_definition}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $dq_{sp}=\rho(r)r^{2}sin(\theta)drd\theta d\phi$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:dq_sp"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 into 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:image_charge_dq_definition"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
dq'=-\frac{R}{r_{2}}\rho(r)r^{2}sin(\theta)drd\theta d\phi\label{eq:image_charge_dq_final}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
applying Coulumb's law using 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:image_charge_dq_final"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:r4_direction"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\paragraph_spacing double
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{cc}
d\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}= & \frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\frac{dq'}{r^{2}_{4}}\hat{r}_{4}\\
d\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}= & \frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\frac{-\frac{R}{r_{2}}\rho(r)r^{2}sin(\theta)drd\theta d\phi}{r^{2}_{4}}\cdot\frac{-\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)cos(\phi)\hat{x}-\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)sin(\phi)\hat{y}-\left\{ \frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]-R-z\right\} \hat{z}}{r_{4}}\\
d\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}= & \frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\frac{\frac{R}{r_{2}}\rho(r)r^{2}sin(\theta)}{r^{3}_{4}}\left(\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)cos(\phi)\hat{x}+\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)sin(\phi)\hat{y}+\left\{ \frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]-R-z\right\} \hat{z}\right)d\phi drd\theta
\end{array}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
let:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}=\cancelto{0}{\vec{I}_{rod,1}}+\vec{I}_{rod,2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\vec{I}_{rod,1}=\int^{\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\int^{2\pi}_{0}\frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\frac{\frac{R}{r_{2}}\rho(r)r^{2}sin(\theta)}{r^{3}_{4}}\left(\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)cos(\phi)\hat{x}+\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)sin(\phi)\hat{y}\right)d\phi drd\theta=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\vec{I}_{rod,2}=\int^{\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\int^{2\pi}_{0}\frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\frac{\frac{R}{r_{2}}\rho(r)r^{2}sin(\theta)}{r^{3}_{4}}\left\{ \frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]-R-z\right\} \hat{z}d\phi drd\theta
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
so:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\paragraph_spacing double
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cc}
\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}= & \int^{\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\int^{2\pi}_{0}\frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\frac{\frac{R}{r_{2}}\rho(r)r^{2}sin(\theta)}{r^{3}_{4}}\left\{ \frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]-R-z\right\} \hat{z}d\phi drd\theta\\
\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}= & \frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\cancelto{2\pi}{\int^{2\pi}_{0}d\phi}\;\;\;\;\int^{\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{\frac{R}{r_{2}}\rho(r)r^{2}sin(\theta)}{r^{3}_{4}}\left[r_{3}\frac{R+rcos(\theta)}{r_{2}}-R-z\right]drd\theta\hat{z}\\
\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}= & -\frac{1}{2\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\int^{\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{\frac{R}{r_{2}}\rho(r)r^{2}sin(\theta)}{r^{3}_{4}}\left[z+R-r_{3}\frac{R+rcos(\theta)}{r_{2}}\right]drd\theta\hat{z}\\
\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}= & -\frac{1}{2\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\int^{\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{R\rho(r)r^{2}sin(\theta)}{r^{2}_{4}r_{2}}\cdot\frac{z+R-r_{3}cos(\theta_{3})}{r_{4}}drd\theta\hat{z}\\
\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}= & -\frac{1}{2\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\int^{\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{Rr^{2}\rho(r)sin(\theta)}{r^{2}_{4}r_{2}}cos(\theta_{4})drd\theta\hat{z}
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\paragraph_spacing double
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}=-\frac{1}{2\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\int^{\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{Rr^{2}\rho(r)sin(\theta)cos(\theta_{4})}{r^{2}_{4}r_{2}}drd\theta\hat{z}\label{eq:Erod_theta3_theta4}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{OR}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}=\frac{1}{2\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\int^{\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{\rho(r)r^{2}R\cdot sin(\theta)}{r^{3}_{4}r_{2}}\left[r_{3}\frac{R+rcos(\theta)}{r_{2}}-R-z\right]drd\theta\hat{z}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Conclusion 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Primary Formulation:
 Pure Coordinate Representation (Computational Core)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For numerical implementation and discrete simulations,
 expressing the image electric field directly in the global coordinate system avoids intermediate trigonometric transformations and sign ambiguities.
 The field is evaluated directly via:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\paragraph_spacing double
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
r_{4}=\sqrt{\left(z+R-\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]\right)^{2}+\left(\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)\right)^{2}}\\
\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}=-\frac{1}{2\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\int^{\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{\rho(r)r^{2}R\cdot sin(\theta)}{r^{3}_{4}r_{2}}\left[z+R-\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}\left(R+rcos(\theta)\right)\right]drd\theta\hat{z}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Alternative Formulation:
 Intermediate Angle Representation (Wang & Wang Framework)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For analytical comparison with classical literature (e.g.,
 Wang & Wang),
 the geometry can alternatively be parameterized using the polar transformation angles 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{3}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{4}$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\paragraph_spacing double
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
r_{4}=\sqrt{\left[z+R-r_{3}cos(\theta_{3})\right]^{2}+\left[r_{3}sin(\theta_{3})\right]^{2}}\\
\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}=-\frac{1}{2\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\int^{\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{Rr^{2}\rho(r)sin(\theta)cos(\theta_{4})}{r^{2}_{4}r_{2}}drd\theta\hat{z}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Note on Implementation
\series default
:
 While both formulations are mathematically identical,
 the pure coordinate representation is preferred in computational algorithms to minimize floating-point operation overhead (eliminating 
\begin_inset Formula $cos^{-1}$
\end_inset

 ,
 
\begin_inset Formula $sin^{-1}$
\end_inset

 calls) and preserve strict sign consistency along the 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}$
\end_inset

 axis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Calculation Of The Plane Image Electric Field (
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{I_{plane}}$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:Vector-schematic-for-plane-image"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 illustrates the schematic vector layout used to evaluate the electrostatic field contribution originating from the image charge of the grounded boundary plane located at 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{P}_{plane}=d\hat{z}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

 is the gap distance measured from the rod apex.
 The differential element (
\begin_inset Formula $dq_{sp}$
\end_inset

) within the localized space charge distribution is specified at spatial coordinate 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{P}_{dq_{sp}}=rsin(\theta)cos(\phi)\hat{x}+rsin(\theta)sin(\phi)\hat{y}+rcos(\theta)\hat{z}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:space_charge_pos"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 via spherical representation relative to the conductor tip (apex),
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 are the internal local cloud coordinates.
 All remaining spatial distribution parameters retain their previously defined definitions.
\end_layout

