\begin_layout Section
Dynamic Kinematics and Relative Coordinate Tracking
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
alignment document
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename assets/v2/space_charge_kinematics.pdf
	scale 60

\end_inset


\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Space Charge Kinematics
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To incorporate the spatial translation of the drifting space charge cloud along the primary axis of symmetry,
 the kinematics are integrated into the existing framework using a 
\series bold
continuous relative coordinate mapping
\series default
.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{\text{drift}}(t)$
\end_inset

 represent the time-dependent,
 absolute physical translation of the whole space charge cloud,
 which moves as a rigid body,
 along the positive 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}$
\end_inset

 direction away from its initial coordinate.
 Every charge element is translated by the same distance,
 and so is the reference point 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{c}$
\end_inset

 where the cloud's own field vanishes.
 Following [1],
 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{c}$
\end_inset

 is defined as the zero of 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset

,
 not as the centre of mass of the charge:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{\text{c}}(t)=z_{\text{c},0}+z_{\text{drift}}(t)\;\;\;\;,\;\;\;\;E_{\text{sp}}(z_{c,0})=0\implies z_{c,0}=E^{-1}_{\text{sp}}(0)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because the governing electrostatic field equations depend strictly on the relative displacement vector directed from the source charge coordinate to the target evaluation point (
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{P}_{observer}-\vec{P}_{charge,source}$
\end_inset

),
 the physical vertical tracking component transforms as follows:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\text{Axial Displacement} & =z-z_{\text{c}}(t)\nonumber \\
 & =z-\left(z_{\text{c},0}+z_{\text{drift}}(t)\right)\nonumber \\
 & =\left(z-z_{\text{drift}}(t)\right)-z_{\text{c},0}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This algebraic grouping demonstrates that physically moving the space charge cloud forward by 
\begin_inset Formula $+z_{\text{drift}}(t)$
\end_inset

 is mathematically identical to keeping the cloud’s integration grid completely stationary and shifting the effective observation coordinate backward by 
\begin_inset Formula $-z_{\text{drift}}(t)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consequently,
 the simulation engine preserves the static computational geometry of the mesh grid.
 To evaluate the resulting fields at an absolute location 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

,
 the framework simply passes a time-dependent effective observer coordinate into the static integral of the space charge field 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{sp}$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{\text{eff}}(t)=z-z_{\text{drift}}(t)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This spatial substitution variable replaces the literal observer coordinate 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 in the space charge field 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{sp}$
\end_inset

,
 eliminating the computational overhead of dynamically rebuilding or translating multi-dimensional arrays during runtime.
 It does not apply to the image fields 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{I_{plane}}$
\end_inset

,
 which also depend on the fixed electrodes;
 their treatment under drift is derived in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:drift_image_fields"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Kinematic Time-Stepping and Discrete Integration
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The dynamic translation 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{\text{drift}}(t)$
\end_inset

 of the space charge cloud is governed by the time-varying total driving electric field evaluated at the moving reference point 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{c}(t)$
\end_inset

.
 By framing the translation explicitly as a continuous function of time,
 the total accumulated drift distance at any instant 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is resolved directly by integrating the cloud’s velocity:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{\text{drift}}(t)=\int^{t}_{0}\underbrace{\mu^{+}\left[E_{\text{ex}}\left(\;z_{c}(\tau)\;\right)+E_{\text{I,rod}}\left(\;z_{c}(\tau)\;\right)+E_{\text{I,plane}}\left(\;z_{c}(\tau)\;\right)\right]}_{\left\Vert \vec{v}\right\Vert \;\left[m/s\right]:\;\text{The drift speed of the cloud}}d\tau\;\;\left[m\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where the mobility coefficient for positive ions in atmospheric air is taken from [1] (
\begin_inset Formula $b=1.36\times10^{-3}\,\text{cm}^{2}/(\text{kV}\cdot\mu\text{s})$
\end_inset

):
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mu^{+}=1.36\times10^{-4}\quad\left[\frac{\text{m}^{2}}{\text{V}\cdot\text{s}}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Decoupled Coordinate Mapping and Kinematic Reference Tracking
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To progress the system chronologically from time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $t+\Delta t$
\end_inset

,
 the continuous integration limits are partitioned according to the additive property of definite integrals:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{\text{drift}}(t+\Delta t)=z_{\text{drift}}(t)+\int^{t+\Delta t}_{t}\mu^{+}\left[E_{\text{ex}}\left(\;z_{c}(\tau)\;\right)+E_{\text{I,rod}}\left(\;z_{c}(\tau)\;\right)+E_{\text{I,plane}}\left(\;z_{c}(\tau)\;\right)\right]d\tau
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second term on the right-hand side represents the exact incremental spatial displacement 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{m}(t)$
\end_inset

 
\series bold
[1]
\series default
 over the step interval 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t$
\end_inset

.
 Defining this step displacement yields:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{m}(t)=\int^{t+\Delta t}_{t}\mu^{+}\left[E_{\text{ex}}\left(\;z_{c}(\tau)\;\right)+E_{\text{I,rod}}\left(\;z_{c}(\tau)\;\right)+E_{\text{I,plane}}\left(\;z_{c}(\tau)\;\right)\right]d\tau
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Substituting this definition back into the partitioned integral yields the clean time-dependent kinematic evolution law:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{\text{drift}}(t+\Delta t)=z_{\text{drift}}(t)+z_{m}(t)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This mathematical formulation bridges continuous classical kinematics with discrete computational execution.
 The simulation engine simply advances a master time variable 
\begin_inset Formula $t\to t+\Delta t$
\end_inset

,
 which inherently maps the updated spatial boundaries for 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{\text{drift}}(t)$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{c}(t)$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{\text{eff}}(t)$
\end_inset

 without relying on software-specific accumulator variables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Applying the Drift to the Image Fields
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:drift_image_fields"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The relative coordinate mapping 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{\text{eff}}(t)=z-z_{\text{drift}}(t)$
\end_inset

 of the previous subsections is exact for the space charge field 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{sp}$
\end_inset

 only.
 Eq.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:final_Esp"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 depends solely on the displacement between a source element and the observer,
 and no other body takes part,
 so translating the cloud is the same as translating the observer backwards.
 The image fields 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{I_{plane}}$
\end_inset

 are different:
 they also depend on where the charge sits relative to the rod and the grounded plane,
 and 
\series bold
the electrodes do not move
\series default
 during the drift.
 Shifting the observer would implicitly drag the rod and the plane along with the cloud.
 For the image fields the drift must therefore be applied to the 
\series bold
source position
\series default
,
 while the electrodes stay fixed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Translated Source Position
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
During the dark period the cloud moves as a rigid body,
 so every charge element is translated by the same distance.
 Measured from the apex 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{O}$
\end_inset

 of the rod tip,
 the axial position of a source element becomes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{dq}(t)=r\cos(\theta)+z_{\text{drift}}(t),\label{eq:drift_source_position}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
while its radial position 
\begin_inset Formula $r\sin(\theta)$
\end_inset

,
 the density 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho(r)$
\end_inset

 and the integration domains are unchanged.
 Every field of the space charge follows by replacing 
\begin_inset Formula $r\cos(\theta)$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $r\cos(\theta)+z_{\text{drift}}(t)$
\end_inset

 wherever the source position appears.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Space Charge Field
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{sp}$
\end_inset

 the substitution gives
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{1}(t)=\sqrt{\left[z-r\cos(\theta)-z_{\text{drift}}(t)\right]^{2}+\left[r\sin(\theta)\right]^{2}},
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which is 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{1}$
\end_inset

 evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{\text{eff}}(t)$
\end_inset

.
 The source-translation view therefore reproduces the result of the relative coordinate mapping exactly:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{sp}(z,t)=\vec{E}_{sp}\left(z_{\text{eff}}(t)\right)$
\end_inset

,
 i.e.
 the curve is shifted by 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{\text{drift}}(t)$
\end_inset

 without changing shape.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Rod Image Field
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The centre 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{C}$
\end_inset

 of the rod tip does not move,
 so the distance between a source element and 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{C}$
\end_inset

 grows with the drift:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{2}(t)=\sqrt{\left[R+r\cos(\theta)+z_{\text{drift}}(t)\right]^{2}+\left[r\sin(\theta)\right]^{2}},\qquad r_{3}(t)=\frac{R^{2}}{r_{2}(t)},\label{eq:drift_r2_r3}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{4}(t)=\sqrt{\left(z+R-\frac{r_{3}(t)}{r_{2}(t)}\left[R+r\cos(\theta)+z_{\text{drift}}(t)\right]\right)^{2}+\left(\frac{r_{3}(t)}{r_{2}(t)}r\sin(\theta)\right)^{2}},
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}(z,t)=-\frac{1}{2\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\int^{\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{\rho(r)r^{2}R\sin(\theta)}{r^{3}_{4}(t)\,r_{2}(t)}\left[z+R-\frac{r_{3}(t)}{r_{2}(t)}\left(R+r\cos(\theta)+z_{\text{drift}}(t)\right)\right]dr\,d\theta\,\hat{z}\label{eq:drift_E_rod}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two effects make this field change shape rather than shift.
 First,
 the Kelvin image of a source element lies at 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(R/r_{2}\right)^{2}\left[R+r\cos(\theta)+z_{\text{drift}}\right]$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{C}$
\end_inset

;
 as the cloud moves away,
 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{2}$
\end_inset

 grows and the image moves 
\series bold
towards
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{C}$
\end_inset

,
 not along with the cloud.
 Second,
 the image charge is 
\begin_inset Formula $-dq\,R/r_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 so it becomes 
\series bold
weaker
\series default
 as the cloud recedes.
 Applying 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{\text{eff}}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}$
\end_inset

 instead would keep the image rigidly attached to the cloud,
 which describes a rod that moves with the space charge.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Plane Image Field
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The grounded plane at 
\begin_inset Formula $z=d$
\end_inset

 (measured from the apex 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{O}$
\end_inset

) does not move either.
 The mirror image of a source element lies at 
\begin_inset Formula $2d-\left[r\cos(\theta)+z_{\text{drift}}(t)\right]$
\end_inset

,
 so it moves by 
\begin_inset Formula $-z_{\text{drift}}$
\end_inset

,
 i.e.
 
\series bold
towards
\series default
 the cloud:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{5}(t)=\sqrt{\left[r\sin(\theta)\right]^{2}+\left(2d-r\cos(\theta)-z_{\text{drift}}(t)-z\right)^{2}},
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\vec{E}_{I_{plane}}(z,t)=\frac{1}{2\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\int^{\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{\rho(r)r^{2}\sin(\theta)}{r^{3}_{5}(t)}\left(2d-r\cos(\theta)-z_{\text{drift}}(t)-z\right)dr\,d\theta\,\hat{z}\label{eq:drift_E_plane}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{\text{eff}}$
\end_inset

 here would give 
\begin_inset Formula $+z_{\text{drift}}$
\end_inset

 inside the bracket,
 moving the image in the wrong direction.
 In long gaps this field is very small,
 but the formulation must still be consistent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Applied Field
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The applied field 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{ext}$
\end_inset

 depends only on the electrode geometry and the applied voltage.
 It is independent of the space charge and therefore 
\series bold
does not change
\series default
 during the drift.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Consequence for the Driving Field
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the drift integral for 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{\text{drift}}(t)$
\end_inset

,
 the image fields 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\text{I,rod}}\left(z_{c}(\tau)\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\text{I,plane}}\left(z_{c}(\tau)\right)$
\end_inset

 are the images of the cloud 
\series bold
at its drifted position
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{\text{drift}}(\tau)$
\end_inset

,
 i.e.
 they are evaluated with Eqs.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:drift_E_rod"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:drift_E_plane"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

.
 The space charge field is absent from the driving field on purpose:
 a charge distribution exerts no net force on itself,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{c}$
\end_inset

 is by definition the point where 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}\left(z_{c}\right)=0$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{sp}$
\end_inset

 moves rigidly with the cloud,
 its zero stays at 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{c}(t)=z_{c,0}+z_{\text{drift}}(t)$
\end_inset

 throughout the drift.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Summary
\end_layout

