\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Conclusion.
\series default
 Our implementation follows the paper's equations (Section 3) and reproduces the shape, sign, order of magnitude and characteristic points of Figure 3. The quantitative difference, mainly in the rod image field near the tip, cannot be removed by any physically consistent choice of the unreported parameters, so it must come from a detail of the authors' calculation that the paper does not report. The reproduction is therefore considered qualitatively validated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Figure 4: Drift of the Space Charge
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Figure 4 of [1] shows 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 before and after the space charge has drifted towards the plane during the dark period. The paper gives the drift equation and the ion mobility, but not the drift time. The two curves of the figure show a drift of about 
\begin_inset Formula $0.07\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
: the minimum of the drifted curve, where the apex of the moved cone now sits, lies at 
\begin_inset Formula $z\approx0.07\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
. The cloud of Figure 3 was therefore drifted by 
\begin_inset Formula $0.07\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 as a rigid body (Section 4), with 
\begin_inset Formula $b=1.36\times10^{-4}\,\text{m}^{2}/(\text{V}\cdot\text{s})$
\end_inset
 and the applied voltage held at 
\begin_inset Formula $27.5\,\text{kV}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

